<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bentheim Castle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bentheim Castle is a hilltop castle in Bad Bentheim, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, built on a sandstone ridge near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> border. First recorded in the late 11th or early 12th century, it was the seat of the counts of Bentheim and is one of the largest castles in north-west Germany. It still belongs to the princely family of Bentheim and Steinfurt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bentheim-castle.jpg" alt="Bentheim Castle" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bad_Bentheim,_Burg_Bentheim_--_2014_--_9555_--_Ausschnitt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The castle stands on an outcrop of Bentheim sandstone, the Bentheimer Höhenrücken, the last protrusion of the nearby Teutoburger forest. The stone supplied the castle's own building material and was also a valued export. Its height above an otherwise very flat landscape gives a wide view and made the site a strong strategic position. It was raised on the remains of an earlier refuge castle, whose history is largely unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1116, in the war between Lothar of Süpplinburg, later Lothar III, and Heinrich V, the castle was destroyed; the Annalista Saxo records that Lothar besieged and burned Binithem, a fine and strong city. It was soon rebuilt, this time in stone, by Otto von Salm, Lothar's brother-in-law. In 1146 Otto von Rheineck was defeated near Ootmarsum in a war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Utrecht over Twente, and the castle became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the Diocese of Utrecht.</p><p>Through Sophie von Salm-Rheineck's marriage to Dirk VI, Count of Holland, castle and county passed to the Counts of Holland, whose line of Bentheim-Holland held them until 1421, when they went to Eberwin IV von Götterswick. The independent county troubled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Münster and Utrecht, who both attacked the castle in 1374. In 1486 Eberwin II received the county as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> from Frederick III, making it an independent political entity, and work on the Pulverturm began in 1489.</p><p>Between 1588 and 1593 Arnold II took the side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, following Calvin and Zwingli, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> the county was repeatedly sacked by Spanish troops and much of the castle destroyed. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> it was besieged and taken several times by French and British troops, and in 1795, while serving as a field hospital, it was set ablaze and captured by General Dominique Vandamme.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bentheim Castle, an early medieval hill castle in the town of Bad Bentheim, belongs to the Prince of Bentheim-Steinfurt, who lives at Steinfurt Castle. It is home to some of his siblings and is open to the public as a family museum, and its gardens have hosted Knight Games since 2006.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bentheim Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bentheim Castle</b> appears around 1050 to 1116 on a sandstone ridge on the Vechte river near the Dutch border.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-bentheim">County of Bentheim</a></li></ul></section>
